Figure 1 plots every pair of measurements. Setosa forms its own cluster in
every panel that involves a petal measurement. Versicolor and virginica
overlap in every panel, though least in the petal panels, where virginica sits
up and to the right. The measurements are also strongly correlated within
species: a longer sepal tends to go with a wider one, and a longer petal with a
wider one. This is why LDA helps. A weighted combination can separate groups
that overlap on each measurement taken alone.

```{r, file="figs/pairs.png", width=7, height=7, units=in, res=150}
pal <- c(setosa = "#1b9e77", versicolor = "#d95f02", virginica = "#7570b3")
par(oma = c(4, 1, 1, 1))
pairs(iris[, 1:4], col = adjustcolor(pal[iris$Species], 0.75), pch = 19, cex = 0.6,
      labels = c("Sepal length", "Sepal width", "Petal length", "Petal width"),
      gap = 0.4, oma = c(7, 3, 3, 3))
par(fig = c(0, 1, 0, 1), oma = c(0, 0, 0, 0), mar = c(0, 0, 0, 0), new = TRUE)
plot.new()
legend("bottom", legend = paste("I.", names(pal)), col = pal, pch = 19,
       horiz = TRUE, bty = "n", text.font = 3, inset = 0.01)
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figs/pairs.png)
// result_end
\caption(Scatterplot matrix of the four measurements (cm), coloured by species)
\alttext(A four-by-four grid of scatterplots. Setosa separates from the other two species in every panel that involves petal length or petal width; versicolor and virginica overlap in every panel.)

Figure 2 shows each measurement's distribution by species. The petal
measurements separate setosa from the others completely. They also separate
versicolor from virginica better than the sepal measurements do, although the
two still overlap. The spread differs between species too, especially in the
petals, where setosa varies least. This matters for the equal-covariance
assumption tested below.

```{r, file="figs/univariate.png", width=8, height=5.5, units=in, res=150}
library(ggplot2)
pal <- c(setosa = "#1b9e77", versicolor = "#d95f02", virginica = "#7570b3")
long <- data.frame(Species = rep(iris$Species, 4), stack(iris[, 1:4]))
long$ind <- factor(long$ind, labels = c("Sepal length", "Sepal width",
                                        "Petal length", "Petal width"))
p <- ggplot(long, aes(Species, values, fill = Species)) +
  geom_violin(alpha = 0.3, colour = NA, scale = "width") +
  geom_boxplot(width = 0.18, outlier.shape = NA, fill = "white", alpha = 0.8) +
  geom_jitter(aes(colour = Species), width = 0.07, height = 0, size = 0.8, alpha = 0.6) +
  facet_wrap(~ ind, scales = "free_y") +
  scale_fill_manual(values = pal) + scale_colour_manual(values = pal) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Centimetres") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 13) +
  theme(legend.position = "none", axis.text.x = element_text(face = "italic"),
        strip.text = element_text(face = "bold"))
print(p)
```
