> Methods

>> Fisher's linear discriminant

Suppose $n$ observations $\mathbf{x}_i \in \mathbb{R}^p$ fall into $K$ known
groups, with $n_k$ observations in group $k$, group means $\bar{\mathbf{x}}_k$
and overall mean $\bar{\mathbf{x}}$. The total scatter of the data splits into
the scatter within groups and the scatter between them. The within-groups and
between-groups sums of squares and cross-products (SSCP) matrices are
$$
  \mathbf{W} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} \sum_{i \in k} (\mathbf{x}_i - \bar{\mathbf{x}}_k)(\mathbf{x}_i - \bar{\mathbf{x}}_k)^\top,
  \qquad
  \mathbf{B} = \sum_{k=1}^{K} n_k (\bar{\mathbf{x}}_k - \bar{\mathbf{x}})(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_k - \bar{\mathbf{x}})^\top .
$$
\alttext(The within-groups matrix W sums outer products of deviations from each group mean; the between-groups matrix B sums n_k times outer products of group-mean deviations from the grand mean.)

\cite(fisher1936) looked for the linear combination
$z = \mathbf{a}^\top \mathbf{x}$ whose group means are as far apart as possible
relative to the spread within groups. That is, he maximized the ratio
$$
  J(\mathbf{a}) = \frac{\mathbf{a}^\top \mathbf{B}\,\mathbf{a}}{\mathbf{a}^\top \mathbf{W}\,\mathbf{a}} .
$$
\alttext(J of a equals a-transpose B a divided by a-transpose W a.)

Setting the derivative of $J$ to zero gives the generalized eigenproblem
$$
  \mathbf{B}\,\mathbf{a} = \lambda\, \mathbf{W}\,\mathbf{a},
$$
\alttext(B a equals lambda W a.)
so the best direction $\mathbf{a}_1$ is the eigenvector of
$\mathbf{W}^{-1}\mathbf{B}$ with the largest eigenvalue $\lambda_1$, and
$J(\mathbf{a}_1) = \lambda_1$. The eigenvectors that follow give further
discriminant functions. Each maximizes $J$ among directions whose scores are
uncorrelated within groups with the scores of the earlier functions.
$\mathbf{B}$ has rank at most $K - 1$, so there are
$s = \min(K - 1, p)$ functions with non-zero eigenvalues. For the iris data,
$K = 3$ and $p = 4$, so the four measurements reduce to two discriminant
functions, LD1 and LD2, without losing any information that separates the
groups.

An eigenvector's length is arbitrary. R's `MASS::lda` \citep(venables2002)
scales the functions so that each has unit variance within groups, which
makes the discriminant space Euclidean: distances there are Mahalanobis
distances in the original measurements. The sign of each function is also
arbitrary, so software may flip it.

>> The probabilistic model

The same functions come out of a probability model. Suppose that within group
$k$ the measurements are multivariate normal, with a mean of their own and a
covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ shared by all groups,
$$
  \mathbf{x} \mid G = k \;\sim\; N_p(\boldsymbol{\mu}_k, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}),
$$
\alttext(Given group k, x is p-variate normal with mean mu k and common covariance Sigma.)
and that group $k$ has prior probability $\pi_k$. Bayes' theorem gives the
posterior probability that an observation belongs to group $k$,
$$
  P(G = k \mid \mathbf{x}) = \frac{\pi_k f_k(\mathbf{x})}{\sum_{j=1}^{K} \pi_j f_j(\mathbf{x})},
$$
\alttext(The posterior probability of group k is pi k times the density f k at x, divided by the sum over all groups of the same product.)
where $f_k$ is the normal density. Because $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ is the same for
every group, the quadratic terms in $\mathbf{x}$ cancel when groups are
compared. What remains is a linear discriminant score
$$
  \delta_k(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^\top \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\mu}_k
  - \frac{1}{2} \boldsymbol{\mu}_k^\top \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\mu}_k + \log \pi_k ,
$$
\alttext(delta k of x equals x-transpose Sigma-inverse mu k, minus one half mu k-transpose Sigma-inverse mu k, plus log pi k.)
and the Bayes rule assigns $\mathbf{x}$ to the group with the largest
$\delta_k(\mathbf{x})$. The boundary between two groups, where their scores are
equal, is therefore a hyperplane \citep(hastie2009). In practice the group means
are estimated by the sample means, and $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ by the pooled
within-groups covariance $\hat{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}} = \mathbf{W} / (n - K)$.

The two views agree. When the rule uses all $s$ discriminant functions, it is
the same as assigning each observation to the nearest group centroid in the
discriminant space, adjusted for the priors:
$$
  \hat{G}(\mathbf{x}) = \arg\min_{k} \; \| \mathbf{z} - \bar{\mathbf{z}}_k \|^2 - 2 \log \pi_k ,
$$
\alttext(The predicted group is the k that minimises the squared distance from the discriminant scores z to the group centroid z-bar k, minus twice log pi k.)
where $\mathbf{z}$ holds the observation's discriminant scores and
$\bar{\mathbf{z}}_k$ the centroid of group $k$. Here the priors are the group
proportions, all equal to 1/3, so the rule reduces to the nearest centroid.

>> Assumptions

The probability model assumes that:

- observations are independent;
- within each group, the measurements are multivariate normal;
- every group has the same covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$;
- no measurement is a linear combination of the others, so that
  $\mathbf{W}$ can be inverted.

Fisher's criterion itself needs none of the distributional assumptions: it is a
geometric definition of a good projection. The assumptions matter for the
posterior probabilities, which are only calibrated if the model holds, and for
whether the Bayes rule is actually optimal. The rule usually classifies well
even when the assumptions are moderately violated.

Normality within groups can be checked one variable at a time with the
Shapiro-Wilk test. Equality of the covariance matrices is tested with Box's M
test \citep(box1949). With $\mathbf{S}_k$ the covariance matrix of group $k$ and
$\mathbf{S}_p = \mathbf{W}/(n - K)$ the pooled one, the statistic is
$$
  M = (n - K) \log |\mathbf{S}_p| - \sum_{k=1}^{K} (n_k - 1) \log |\mathbf{S}_k| .
$$
\alttext(M equals n minus K times the log determinant of the pooled covariance, minus the sum over groups of n k minus one times the log determinant of that group's covariance.)
$M$ is zero when all the $\mathbf{S}_k$ are equal and grows as they diverge.
After a small-sample correction, $M$ is approximately $\chi^2$ with
$p(p+1)(K-1)/2$ degrees of freedom. The test is known to be very sensitive,
both to departures from normality and to differences in covariance too small to
matter for classification. For that reason we treat it as a diagnostic, and
compare LDA with quadratic discriminant analysis (QDA), which estimates a
separate covariance matrix for each group and does not need the assumption.

>> Interpreting the discriminant functions

A fitted discriminant is described by several quantities:

- *Eigenvalues* $\lambda_j$: the between-to-within variance ratio achieved by
  each function. Their shares of the total, $\lambda_j / \sum_i \lambda_i$
  (the "proportion of trace"), say how much of the group separation each
  function carries.
- *Canonical correlations* $r_j = \sqrt{\lambda_j / (1 + \lambda_j)}$: the
  correlation between function $j$ and the best linear combination of group
  indicators.
- *Dimension tests*: Wilks'
  $\Lambda_j = \prod_{i=j}^{s} (1 + \lambda_i)^{-1}$ tests whether functions
  $j, \dots, s$ carry any separation. Bartlett's approximation
  $\chi^2 = -\left(n - 1 - \frac{p + K}{2}\right) \log \Lambda_j$ has
  $(p - j + 1)(K - j)$ degrees of freedom \citep(mardia1979). For $j = 1$ this
  is the MANOVA test of equal group means.
- *Raw coefficients* $\mathbf{a}_j$: the weights that turn measurements into
  scores. They depend on the measurement units.
- *Standardised coefficients* $a_{jl} s_l$, where $s_l$ is the pooled
  within-groups standard deviation of variable $l$. These are comparable across
  variables and show each variable's contribution to the function, given the
  other variables.
- *Structure correlations*: the correlation between each measurement and each
  function's scores. These say what a function "is" in terms of the original
  variables. Unlike the standardised coefficients, they are not distorted by
  correlation among the predictors.

>> Measuring classification accuracy

The error rate on the data used to fit the model (the *resubstitution* or
apparent error) is optimistic, because the model has been tuned to exactly
those flowers. We therefore also estimate the error in two honest ways:

+ *Leave-one-out cross-validation* \citep(lachenbruch1968): each flower in
  turn is classified by a discriminant fitted to the other 149.
  `MASS::lda(..., CV = TRUE)` does this without refitting the model 150 times.
+ *Repeated hold-out*: 500 times, fit to 35 random flowers per species and
  classify the other 15 per species. This shows how much the error varies with
  the particular training sample.

>> Software

All computations use R 4.6.1 \citep(rcore2026). The discriminant and QDA come
from the `MASS` package \citep(venables2002), most figures are drawn with
`ggplot2` \citep(wickham2016), and the tables are printed as Markdown by
`knitr::kable()` \citep(xie2015). Box's M test is computed directly from its
definition above. R has no built-in function for it, and implementing it here
keeps the report free of extra dependencies.

> Analysis

>> Do the species differ?

Classifying the flowers only makes sense if the species differ in their
measurements. A one-way multivariate analysis of variance (MANOVA) tests the
hypothesis that the three species have the same mean vector.

All four standard MANOVA statistics reject equal means overwhelmingly (Table 5).
Wilks' $\Lambda = 0.023$ means that only 2.3% of the generalised variance in the
measurements is left unexplained by species.

```{r, results=markdown}
fit <- manova(as.matrix(iris[, 1:4]) ~ Species, data = iris)
tests <- c(Wilks = "Wilks' $\\Lambda$", Pillai = "Pillai's trace",
           "Hotelling-Lawley" = "Hotelling-Lawley trace", Roy = "Roy's largest root")
stats <- t(sapply(names(tests), function(test) summary(fit, test = test)$stats[1, 2:6]))
sci <- function(p) {  # p-values as a x 10^b, in mepml maths
  e <- floor(log10(p))
  sprintf("$%.1f \\times 10^{%d}$", p / 10^e, e)
}
knitr::kable(data.frame(tests, sprintf("%.4f", stats[, 1]), sprintf("%.1f", stats[, 2]),
                        stats[, 3], stats[, 4], sci(stats[, 5])),
             col.names = c("Statistic", "Value", "Approx. F", "df1", "df2", "p-value"),
             align = "lrrrrr", row.names = FALSE)
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Statistic              |   Value | Approx. F | df1 | df2 |                p-value |
| :--------------------- | ------: | --------: | --: | --: | ---------------------: |
| Wilks' $\Lambda$       |  0.0234 |     199.1 |   8 | 288 | $1.4 \times 10^{-112}$ |
| Pillai's trace         |  1.1919 |      53.5 |   8 | 290 |  $9.7 \times 10^{-53}$ |
| Hotelling-Lawley trace | 32.4773 |     580.5 |   8 | 286 | $6.4 \times 10^{-172}$ |
| Roy's largest root     | 32.1919 |    1167.0 |   4 | 145 | $3.8 \times 10^{-109}$ |
// result_end
\caption(One-way MANOVA of the four measurements on species)

>> Checking the assumptions

The flowers were measured separately, so we take them to be independent, and
none of the measurements is a linear combination of the others. That leaves
normality and equal covariances, which we check with a Shapiro-Wilk test on
each measurement within each species and with Box's M test.

Table 6 lists the Shapiro-Wilk p-values. Eleven of the twelve distributions look
compatible with normality at the 1% level. Setosa's petal width does not
($p < 0.001$). Its values are recorded to the nearest millimetre and 29 of the
50 are exactly 0.2 cm, so the distribution is lumpy and skewed to the right. The
same coarse recording shows as rows of overlapping points in Figure 1.
Versicolor's petal width is borderline ($p = 0.027$). Neither departure is
serious for classification. The one clear failure is in setosa, a species
separated from the others by a gap far larger than the width of its
distribution.

```{r, results=markdown}
p <- sapply(split(iris[, 1:4], iris$Species),
            function(d) sapply(d, function(v) shapiro.test(v)$p.value))
cells <- ifelse(p < 0.001, "< 0.001", sprintf("%.3f", p))
knitr::kable(data.frame(c("Sepal length", "Sepal width", "Petal length", "Petal width"), cells),
             col.names = c("Measurement", paste0("~I. ", colnames(p), "~")),
             align = "lrrr", row.names = FALSE)
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Measurement  | ~I. setosa~ | ~I. versicolor~ | ~I. virginica~ |
| :----------- | ----------: | --------------: | -------------: |
| Sepal length |       0.460 |           0.465 |          0.258 |
| Sepal width  |       0.272 |           0.338 |          0.181 |
| Petal length |       0.055 |           0.158 |          0.110 |
| Petal width  |     < 0.001 |           0.027 |          0.087 |
// result_end
\caption(Shapiro-Wilk p-values for normality of each measurement within each species)

Equal covariances are clearly rejected (Table 7). The log-determinant of a
covariance matrix measures the volume of the cloud of points. Setosa's is
about 4.1 lower than virginica's, a factor of $e^{4.1} \approx 60$ in
generalised variance: virginica's flowers vary much more than setosa's.

```{r, results=markdown}
# Box's M test of equal covariance matrices (Box, 1949)
box_m <- function(X, g) {
  groups <- split(X, g)
  k <- length(groups); p <- ncol(X)
  n <- sapply(groups, nrow); N <- sum(n)
  S <- lapply(groups, cov)
  Sp <- Reduce(`+`, Map(function(s, m) (m - 1) * s, S, n)) / (N - k)
  logdet <- sapply(S, function(s) determinant(s)$modulus)
  M <- (N - k) * c(determinant(Sp)$modulus) - sum((n - 1) * logdet)
  c1 <- (sum(1 / (n - 1)) - 1 / (N - k)) * (2 * p^2 + 3 * p - 1) / (6 * (p + 1) * (k - 1))
  df <- p * (p + 1) * (k - 1) / 2
  stat <- M * (1 - c1)
  list(log_det = c(logdet, pooled = c(determinant(Sp)$modulus)),
       M = M, chisq = stat, df = df, p.value = pchisq(stat, df, lower.tail = FALSE))
}
b <- box_m(iris[, 1:4], iris$Species)
sci <- function(p) {
  e <- floor(log10(p))
  sprintf("$%.1f \\times 10^{%d}$", p / 10^e, e)
}
knitr::kable(data.frame(
  c(paste0("$\\log \\det \\mathbf{S}_k$, ~I. ", names(b$log_det)[1:3], "~"),
    "$\\log \\det \\mathbf{S}_p$, pooled", "Box's $M$", "$\\chi^2$ (corrected)", "Degrees of freedom", "p-value"),
  c(sprintf("%.2f", c(b$log_det, b$M, b$chisq)), b$df, sci(b$p.value))),
  col.names = c("Quantity", "Value"), align = "lr")
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Quantity                                  |                 Value |
| :---------------------------------------- | --------------------: |
| $\log \det \mathbf{S}_k$, ~I. setosa~     |                -13.07 |
| $\log \det \mathbf{S}_k$, ~I. versicolor~ |                -10.87 |
| $\log \det \mathbf{S}_k$, ~I. virginica~  |                 -8.93 |
| $\log \det \mathbf{S}_p$, pooled          |                 -9.96 |
| Box's $M$                                 |                146.66 |
| $\chi^2$ (corrected)                      |                140.94 |
| Degrees of freedom                        |                    20 |
| p-value                                   | $3.4 \times 10^{-20}$ |
// result_end
\caption(Box's M test of equal covariance matrices across species)

Figure 3 shows what the test detects. For each species it draws the 95% ellipse
of its own covariance matrix (solid) and of the pooled covariance matrix that
LDA assumes (dashed), centred on that species' mean. For the petals the
difference is large. Setosa's actual ellipse is much smaller than the pooled
one, and virginica's is larger. For the sepals, versicolor's and virginica's
ellipses are close to the pooled one, but setosa's is narrower and steeper,
because its sepal length and width are more strongly correlated (0.74, against
0.53 and 0.46 in the other species). The pooled matrix is a compromise, and the
question for the Validation section is whether the compromise costs any
accuracy.

```{r, file="figs/covariance.png", width=8, height=4.2, units=in, res=150}
library(ggplot2)
pal <- c(setosa = "#1b9e77", versicolor = "#d95f02", virginica = "#7570b3")
ellipse <- function(mu, S, level = 0.95) {
  t <- seq(0, 2 * pi, length.out = 200)
  e <- eigen(S)
  r <- sqrt(qchisq(level, 2))
  pts <- t(mu + r * e$vectors %*% diag(sqrt(e$values)) %*% rbind(cos(t), sin(t)))
  data.frame(x = pts[, 1], y = pts[, 2])
}
pairs_of <- list(Sepal = c("Sepal.Length", "Sepal.Width"),
                 Petal = c("Petal.Length", "Petal.Width"))
pts <- ells <- NULL
for (part in names(pairs_of)) {
  v <- pairs_of[[part]]
  groups <- split(iris[, v], iris$Species)
  pooled <- Reduce(`+`, lapply(groups, function(d) (nrow(d) - 1) * cov(d))) / (150 - 3)
  for (sp in names(groups)) {
    mu <- colMeans(groups[[sp]])
    ells <- rbind(ells,
      data.frame(ellipse(mu, cov(groups[[sp]])), Species = sp, part = part, kind = "own"),
      data.frame(ellipse(mu, pooled), Species = sp, part = part, kind = "pooled"))
  }
  pts <- rbind(pts, data.frame(x = iris[[v[1]]], y = iris[[v[2]]],
                               Species = iris$Species, part = part))
}
pts$part <- factor(pts$part, levels = names(pairs_of),
                   labels = c("Sepals (length vs width, cm)", "Petals (length vs width, cm)"))
ells$part <- factor(ells$part, levels = names(pairs_of), labels = levels(pts$part))
p <- ggplot() +
  geom_point(data = pts, aes(x, y, colour = Species), alpha = 0.5, size = 1) +
  geom_path(data = ells, aes(x, y, colour = Species, linetype = kind,
                             group = interaction(Species, kind)), linewidth = 0.7) +
  facet_wrap(~ part, scales = "free") +
  scale_colour_manual(values = pal, labels = paste("I.", names(pal))) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c(own = "solid", pooled = "22"),
                        labels = c(own = "Species' own covariance", pooled = "Pooled covariance (LDA)")) +
  labs(x = "Length", y = "Width", colour = NULL, linetype = NULL) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.box = "vertical", legend.margin = margin(0, 0, 0, 0),
        legend.text = element_text(size = 10), strip.text = element_text(face = "bold"))
print(p)
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figs/covariance.png)
// result_end
\caption(95% normal-theory ellipses of each species' own covariance (solid) and of the pooled covariance LDA assumes (dashed), centred on each species' mean)
\alttext(Two scatterplots, sepals and petals, with an ellipse per species drawn twice. For the petals, setosa's own ellipse is far smaller than the pooled one and virginica's is larger; for the sepals the solid and dashed ellipses are close.)

>> Fitting the discriminant

The model uses all four measurements, with priors equal to the species
proportions (1/3 each).

Both discriminant functions are statistically significant (Table 8). They are
not equally important. LD1 has an eigenvalue of 32.2: along it, the spread
between species is 32 times the spread within them, and it carries 99.1% of the
separation. LD2 carries the remaining 0.9%. Its canonical correlation of 0.47
means it does separate the species, but only weakly. With 150 flowers even that
small effect is highly significant ($p = 5.8 \times 10^{-8}$).

```{r, results=markdown}
library(MASS)
fit <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris)
n <- nrow(iris); K <- nlevels(iris$Species); p <- 4
eig <- fit$svd^2 * (K - 1) / (n - K)                # eigenvalues of W^-1 B
wilks <- sapply(1:2, function(j) prod(1 / (1 + eig[j:2])))
chi <- -(n - 1 - (p + K) / 2) * log(wilks)          # Bartlett's dimension tests
df <- (p - 1:2 + 1) * (K - 1:2)
sci <- function(p) {
  e <- floor(log10(p))
  sprintf("$%.1f \\times 10^{%d}$", p / 10^e, e)
}
knitr::kable(data.frame(c("LD1", "LD2"), sprintf("%.3f", eig), sprintf("%.2f", 100 * eig / sum(eig)),
                        sprintf("%.3f", sqrt(eig / (1 + eig))), sprintf("%.4f", wilks),
                        sprintf("%.2f", chi), df, sci(pchisq(chi, df, lower.tail = FALSE))),
             col.names = c("Function", "Eigenvalue", "% of trace", "Canonical corr.", "Wilks' $\\Lambda$",
                           "$\\chi^2$", "df", "p-value"),
             align = "lrrrrrrr")
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Function | Eigenvalue | % of trace | Canonical corr. | Wilks' $\Lambda$ | $\chi^2$ |  df |                p-value |
| :------- | ---------: | ---------: | --------------: | ---------------: | -------: | --: | ---------------------: |
| LD1      |     32.192 |      99.12 |           0.985 |           0.0234 |   546.12 |   8 | $8.9 \times 10^{-113}$ |
| LD2      |      0.285 |       0.88 |           0.471 |           0.7780 |    36.53 |   3 |   $5.8 \times 10^{-8}$ |
// result_end
\caption(Eigenvalues, canonical correlations and Bartlett's dimension tests. The test in row j asks whether functions j onwards carry any separation.)

Table 9 gives the coefficients. The raw coefficients are what `predict()` uses.
Up to centring, an iris's LD1 score is
$0.83\,\mathrm{SL} + 1.53\,\mathrm{SW} - 2.20\,\mathrm{PL} - 2.81\,\mathrm{PW}$
(sepal length, sepal width, petal length, petal width). The standardised
coefficients put the four measurements on a common scale. By them, LD1 is
dominated by petal length ($-0.95$), with contributions from petal width
($-0.58$), sepal width ($+0.52$) and sepal length ($+0.43$). LD2 is mainly
sepal width and petal width, both negative ($-0.74$ and $-0.58$), against
petal length ($+0.40$).

```{r, results=markdown}
library(MASS)
fit <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris)
# standardised: raw coefficients times the pooled within-species SDs
W <- Reduce(`+`, lapply(split(iris[, 1:4], iris$Species), function(d) (nrow(d) - 1) * cov(d)))
std <- fit$scaling * sqrt(diag(W) / (nrow(iris) - 3))
knitr::kable(data.frame(c("Sepal length", "Sepal width", "Petal length", "Petal width"),
                        fit$scaling, std),
             digits = 3, align = "lrrrr", row.names = FALSE,
             col.names = c("Measurement", "LD1 (raw)", "LD2 (raw)", "LD1 (std.)", "LD2 (std.)"))
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Measurement  | LD1 (raw) | LD2 (raw) | LD1 (std.) | LD2 (std.) |
| :----------- | --------: | --------: | ---------: | ---------: |
| Sepal length |     0.829 |    -0.024 |      0.427 |     -0.012 |
| Sepal width  |     1.534 |    -2.165 |      0.521 |     -0.735 |
| Petal length |    -2.201 |     0.932 |     -0.947 |      0.401 |
| Petal width  |    -2.810 |    -2.839 |     -0.575 |     -0.581 |
// result_end
\caption(Raw and standardised discriminant coefficients. Standardised coefficients are the raw ones multiplied by each measurement's pooled within-species standard deviation.)

Sepal length has a positive LD1 coefficient even though virginica, with the
lowest LD1 scores, has the longest sepals. The structure correlations in
Table 10 explain this. Across the whole sample, sepal length correlates at
$-0.79$ with LD1, in the same direction as the petal measurements, because
large flowers are large in every dimension. The positive coefficient is a
correction: once petal size is known, a longer sepal than expected points
towards setosa. This is a standard reason for reading structure correlations
alongside coefficients rather than instead of them. Taken together, the
correlations say that LD1 is essentially a *petal-size* axis. Over the whole
sample, petal length and petal width correlate $-0.99$ and $-0.97$ with it.
LD2 is essentially a *sepal-width* axis ($-0.86$ within species). The signs
are arbitrary, as noted in the Methods. With these signs, setosa scores high
on LD1.

```{r, results=markdown}
library(MASS)
scores <- predict(lda(Species ~ ., data = iris))$x
# within species: correlations once each species is centred on its own mean
centred <- do.call(rbind, lapply(split(data.frame(iris[, 1:4], scores), iris$Species),
                                 function(d) scale(d, scale = FALSE)))
knitr::kable(data.frame(c("Sepal length", "Sepal width", "Petal length", "Petal width"),
                        cor(centred)[1:4, 5:6], cor(iris[, 1:4], scores)),
             digits = 3, align = "lrrrr", row.names = FALSE,
             col.names = c("Measurement", "LD1 (within)", "LD2 (within)", "LD1 (total)", "LD2 (total)"))
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Measurement  | LD1 (within) | LD2 (within) | LD1 (total) | LD2 (total) |
| :----------- | -----------: | -----------: | ----------: | ----------: |
| Sepal length |       -0.223 |       -0.311 |      -0.792 |      -0.218 |
| Sepal width  |        0.119 |       -0.864 |       0.531 |      -0.758 |
| Petal length |       -0.706 |       -0.168 |      -0.985 |      -0.046 |
| Petal width  |       -0.633 |       -0.737 |      -0.973 |      -0.223 |
// result_end
\caption(Structure correlations between each measurement and the discriminant scores: pooled within species, and over all 150 flowers)

The species centroids in the discriminant space are in Table 11. On LD1 they are
widely spaced and in order: setosa at $+7.6$, versicolor at $-1.8$ and
virginica at $-5.8$. The scores have unit variance within species, so on LD1
alone setosa is 9.4 within-species standard deviations from versicolor, while
versicolor and virginica are 4.0 apart. On LD2, versicolor is the odd
one out, at $+0.73$ against about $-0.2$ to $-0.5$ for the others.

```{r, results=markdown}
library(MASS)
scores <- predict(lda(Species ~ ., data = iris))$x
centroids <- aggregate(scores, list(Species = iris$Species), mean)
centroids$Species <- paste0("~I. ", centroids$Species, "~")
knitr::kable(centroids, digits = 3, align = "lrr")
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Species         |    LD1 |    LD2 |
| :-------------- | -----: | -----: |
| ~I. setosa~     |  7.608 | -0.215 |
| ~I. versicolor~ | -1.825 |  0.728 |
| ~I. virginica~  | -5.783 | -0.513 |
// result_end
\caption(Species centroids in the discriminant space)

>> The discriminant space

Figure 4 plots every flower's scores on the two discriminant functions. It is
the best two-dimensional view of the separation between species: no other
linear projection of the four measurements separates them more by Fisher's
criterion. Setosa lies far to the right, alone. Versicolor and virginica lie
next to each other along LD1, and their 95% ellipses overlap in a narrow band
between them. The three flowers that the fitted rule misclassifies (circled)
all sit in that band.

```{r, file="figs/ld_scores.png", width=7.5, height=3.9, units=in, res=150}
library(MASS); library(ggplot2)
pal <- c(setosa = "#1b9e77", versicolor = "#d95f02", virginica = "#7570b3")
fit <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris)
pred <- predict(fit)
d <- data.frame(pred$x, Species = iris$Species, wrong = pred$class != iris$Species)
cent <- aggregate(cbind(LD1, LD2) ~ Species, d, mean)
pct <- round(100 * fit$svd^2 / sum(fit$svd^2), 1)
p <- ggplot(d, aes(LD1, LD2, colour = Species)) +
  stat_ellipse(type = "norm", level = 0.95, linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.7, size = 1.6) +
  geom_point(data = d[d$wrong, ], shape = 21, size = 4.5, stroke = 0.9, colour = "black") +
  geom_point(data = cent, shape = 4, size = 5, stroke = 1.6, colour = "black") +
  scale_colour_manual(values = pal, labels = paste("I.", names(pal))) +
  labs(x = sprintf("LD1 (%.1f%% of trace)", pct[1]),
       y = sprintf("LD2 (%.1f%% of trace)", pct[2]), colour = NULL) +
  coord_equal() +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.text = element_text(face = "italic"))
print(p)
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figs/ld_scores.png)
// result_end
\caption(The flowers in the discriminant space, with 95% ellipses per species, centroids (crosses) and the three flowers the fitted rule misclassifies (circled). The axes are on equal scales.)
\alttext(A scatterplot of LD2 against LD1. Setosa forms a tight cluster around LD1 = 7.6; versicolor and virginica form adjacent clusters around LD1 = -1.8 and -5.8 that touch between LD1 = -3 and -4, where three circled points lie.)

Figure 5 shows the same scores one function at a time. On LD1 the setosa density
does not touch the others. The versicolor and virginica densities overlap only
in their tails. On LD2 all three densities overlap heavily. LD2 does shift
versicolor upward, which is why it helps a little with the versicolor-virginica
boundary, but on its own it could not classify anything.

```{r, file="figs/ld_densities.png", width=8, height=3.8, units=in, res=150}
library(MASS); library(ggplot2)
pal <- c(setosa = "#1b9e77", versicolor = "#d95f02", virginica = "#7570b3")
scores <- predict(lda(Species ~ ., data = iris))$x
d <- data.frame(score = c(scores[, 1], scores[, 2]),
                fn = rep(c("LD1", "LD2"), each = 150),
                Species = rep(iris$Species, 2))
p <- ggplot(d, aes(score, fill = Species, colour = Species)) +
  geom_density(alpha = 0.3, adjust = 1.1) +
  geom_rug(alpha = 0.5, length = unit(0.04, "npc")) +
  facet_wrap(~ fn, scales = "free") +
  scale_fill_manual(values = pal, labels = paste("I.", names(pal))) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = pal, labels = paste("I.", names(pal))) +
  labs(x = "Discriminant score", y = "Density", fill = NULL, colour = NULL) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.text = element_text(face = "italic"),
        strip.text = element_text(face = "bold"))
print(p)
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figs/ld_densities.png)
// result_end
\caption(Kernel density of each species' scores on LD1 and on LD2)
\alttext(Two density panels. On LD1, setosa's density is far to the right and does not overlap the others, while versicolor and virginica overlap slightly in their tails. On LD2, the three densities overlap almost entirely, versicolor sitting slightly higher.)

Figure 6 draws each measurement as an arrow from the origin to its total-sample
structure correlations with the two functions. The petal arrows point almost
straight along LD1, which is what makes LD1 a petal-size axis. Sepal width
points mostly along LD2. Sepal length lies between the two but closer to LD1.
Arrows near the unit circle are almost entirely explained by the two functions.

```{r, file="figs/structure.png", width=5.5, height=5.5, units=in, res=150}
library(MASS)
fit <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris)
r <- cor(iris[, 1:4], predict(fit)$x)
labs <- c("Sepal length", "Sepal width", "Petal length", "Petal width")
par(mar = c(4.5, 4.5, 1, 1))
plot(NA, xlim = c(-1.1, 1.1), ylim = c(-1.1, 1.1), asp = 1,
     xlab = "Correlation with LD1", ylab = "Correlation with LD2")
abline(h = 0, v = 0, col = "grey80")
t <- seq(0, 2 * pi, length.out = 300)
lines(cos(t), sin(t), col = "grey60")
arrows(0, 0, r[, 1], r[, 2], length = 0.1, lwd = 2, col = "#3b528b")
# labels placed by hand: the three left-pointing arrows end close together
text(c(-0.55, 0.58, -0.75, -0.98), c(-0.33, -0.86, 0.08, -0.33), labs, cex = 0.9, col = "#3b528b")
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figs/structure.png)
// result_end
\caption(Structure correlations over the whole sample: each measurement's correlation with LD1 and LD2, inside the unit circle)
\alttext(A correlation circle. Petal length and petal width arrows point left along the LD1 axis almost to the circle; sepal length points left and slightly down; sepal width points right and down, mostly along LD2.)

The classification rule divides the discriminant plane into three regions, one
per species (Figure 7). The priors are equal and the scores have unit variance
within species, so each point belongs to the region of its nearest centroid, and
the boundaries are the perpendicular bisectors between centroids. The regions
shown are therefore exactly the rule `predict()` applies to the four
measurements. The versicolor-virginica boundary crosses the LD1 axis near
$-3.8$. It is tilted about 17 degrees from vertical by the two species'
separation on LD2.

```{r, file="figs/regions.png", width=7.5, height=3.9, units=in, res=150}
library(MASS); library(ggplot2)
pal <- c(setosa = "#1b9e77", versicolor = "#d95f02", virginica = "#7570b3")
fit <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris)
pred <- predict(fit)
d <- data.frame(pred$x, Species = iris$Species)
cent <- as.matrix(aggregate(cbind(LD1, LD2) ~ Species, d, mean)[, -1])
grid <- expand.grid(LD1 = seq(-11, 11, length.out = 400), LD2 = seq(-3.5, 3.5, length.out = 200))
# nearest centroid, adjusted for the (here equal) priors
dist2 <- sapply(1:3, function(k) (grid$LD1 - cent[k, 1])^2 + (grid$LD2 - cent[k, 2])^2 -
                                 2 * log(fit$prior[k]))
grid$region <- factor(levels(iris$Species)[max.col(-dist2)], levels = levels(iris$Species))
# check: the regions agree with predict() at the flowers themselves
stopifnot(all(levels(iris$Species)[max.col(-sapply(1:3, function(k)
  rowSums(sweep(pred$x, 2, cent[k, ])^2)))] == pred$class))
p <- ggplot() +
  geom_raster(data = grid, aes(LD1, LD2, fill = region), alpha = 0.18) +
  geom_point(data = d, aes(LD1, LD2, colour = Species), size = 1.5, alpha = 0.8) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = pal, guide = "none") +
  scale_colour_manual(values = pal, labels = paste("I.", names(pal))) +
  labs(colour = NULL) +
  coord_equal(expand = FALSE) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.text = element_text(face = "italic"))
print(p)
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figs/regions.png)
// result_end
\caption(Classification regions of the fitted rule in the discriminant space (shaded), with each flower coloured by its true species)
\alttext(The discriminant plane split into three shaded regions by straight boundaries: setosa on the right, versicolor in the middle and virginica on the left. Nearly every point lies in its own species' region; a few versicolor and virginica points lie near the boundary between them.)

>> Classification performance

The discriminant classifies 147 of the 150 flowers correctly (Table 12).
Leave-one-out cross-validation gives exactly the same result as
resubstitution: the same three flowers are misclassified, and no others
(Table 13 gives both classifications). Refitting without a flower moves the coefficients too little to change any
other flower's class. The estimated error rate is therefore 2.0%, and it is not
an artefact of testing on the training data. Every setosa is classified
correctly. The errors are two versicolor taken for virginica and one virginica
taken for versicolor.

```{r, results=markdown}
library(MASS)
cv <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris, CV = TRUE)
tab <- table(iris$Species, cv$class)
pct <- function(right, all) sprintf("%.0f%%", 100 * right / all)
rows <- rbind(cbind(unclass(tab), pct(diag(tab), rowSums(tab))),
              c(colSums(tab), pct(sum(diag(tab)), sum(tab))))
knitr::kable(data.frame(c(paste0("~I. ", rownames(tab), "~"), "Total"), rows),
             col.names = c("Actual species", paste0("Predicted ~", colnames(tab), "~"), "Correct"),
             align = "lrrrr", row.names = FALSE)
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Actual species  | Predicted ~setosa~ | Predicted ~versicolor~ | Predicted ~virginica~ | Correct |
| :-------------- | -----------------: | ---------------------: | --------------------: | ------: |
| ~I. setosa~     |                 50 |                      0 |                     0 |    100% |
| ~I. versicolor~ |                  0 |                     48 |                     2 |     96% |
| ~I. virginica~  |                  0 |                      1 |                    49 |     98% |
| Total           |                 50 |                     49 |                    51 |     98% |
// result_end
\caption(Leave-one-out confusion matrix (resubstitution gives the same table))

The three misclassified flowers are borderline (Table 13). Each is a large
versicolor or a small virginica. Flowers 71 and 84 are versicolor with petals
4.8 and 5.1 cm long, well inside virginica's range (4.5 to 6.9 cm). Flower 71
also has the widest petals of any versicolor (1.8 cm). Flower 134 is a
virginica with petals only 1.5 cm wide, close to the narrowest of its
species. None is misclassified with much
confidence: flower 134's posterior probability of versicolor is 0.79, and the
other two go to virginica with probability 0.82 and 0.90.

```{r, results=markdown}
library(MASS)
cv <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris, CV = TRUE)
resub <- predict(lda(Species ~ ., data = iris))$class
# every flower resubstitution misclassifies is one of these (and vice versa)
stopifnot(identical(which(resub != iris$Species), which(cv$class != iris$Species)))
wrong <- which(cv$class != iris$Species)
knitr::kable(data.frame(wrong, iris[wrong, 1:4], paste0("~", iris$Species[wrong], "~"),
                        paste0("~", cv$class[wrong], "~"), paste0("~", resub[wrong], "~"),
                        cv$posterior[wrong, 2:3]),
             digits = 3, align = "rrrrrlllrr", row.names = FALSE,
             col.names = c("Flower", "Sepal L.", "Sepal W.", "Petal L.", "Petal W.", "Species", "Predicted",
                           "Resubst.", "P(versicolor)", "P(virginica)"))
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Flower | Sepal L. | Sepal W. | Petal L. | Petal W. | Species      | Predicted    | Resubst.     | P(versicolor) | P(virginica) |
| -----: | -------: | -------: | -------: | -------: | :----------- | :----------- | :----------- | ------------: | -----------: |
|     71 |      5.9 |      3.2 |      4.8 |      1.8 | ~versicolor~ | ~virginica~  | ~virginica~  |         0.177 |        0.823 |
|     84 |      6.0 |      2.7 |      5.1 |      1.6 | ~versicolor~ | ~virginica~  | ~virginica~  |         0.099 |        0.901 |
|    134 |      6.3 |      2.8 |      5.1 |      1.5 | ~virginica~  | ~versicolor~ | ~versicolor~ |         0.788 |        0.212 |
// result_end
\caption(The misclassified flowers (leave-one-out), with their measurements in cm, the class resubstitution gives them, and their leave-one-out posterior probabilities. P(setosa) is below 0.001 for all three.)

Figure 8 shows the posterior probabilities for every flower, ordered by LD1
score. Setosa's posteriors, on the right, are all essentially 1. Between
virginica on the left and versicolor, the posterior passes from one species to
the other within a narrow band of flowers. These are the only flowers the model
is uncertain about: just 11 of the 150 have a largest posterior below 0.9. The
uncertainty the model reports matches the errors it actually makes.

```{r, file="figs/posterior.png", width=8, height=4, units=in, res=150}
library(MASS); library(ggplot2)
pal <- c(setosa = "#1b9e77", versicolor = "#d95f02", virginica = "#7570b3")
cv <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris, CV = TRUE)
ld1 <- predict(lda(Species ~ ., data = iris))$x[, 1]
ord <- order(ld1)
post <- data.frame(rank = rep(seq_along(ord), 3),
                   prob = c(cv$posterior[ord, ]),
                   class = factor(rep(colnames(cv$posterior), each = 150), levels = names(pal)))
truth <- data.frame(rank = seq_along(ord), Species = iris$Species[ord],
                    wrong = cv$class[ord] != iris$Species[ord])
p <- ggplot() +
  geom_col(data = post, aes(rank, prob, fill = class), width = 1) +
  geom_tile(data = truth, aes(rank, -0.07, fill = Species), height = 0.06, width = 1) +
  geom_point(data = truth[truth$wrong, ], aes(rank, -0.14), shape = 17, size = 2.2) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = pal, labels = paste("I.", names(pal))) +
  scale_y_continuous(breaks = c(0, 0.5, 1)) +
  labs(x = "Flowers, ordered by LD1 score (virginica-like on the left)",
       y = "Posterior probability", fill = NULL) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", legend.text = element_text(face = "italic"),
        panel.grid.major.x = element_blank(), panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
print(p)
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figs/posterior.png)
// result_end
\caption(Leave-one-out posterior probabilities of each species for every flower, ordered by LD1 score. The strip under the bars gives each flower's true species; triangles mark the three misclassified flowers.)
\alttext(A stacked bar chart of posterior probabilities for 150 flowers. From the left, a long run of virginica-coloured bars changes to versicolor-coloured bars over a short band of mixed bars, then a long versicolor run, then the setosa run on the right, whose bars are all fully setosa. Three triangles mark misclassifications inside the mixed band.)

>> Validation and comparison with other rules

Leave-one-out gives a single estimate of the error rate. Repeated hold-out
shows how much that estimate varies with the particular flowers used for
fitting. We fit three rules to each of 500 stratified random
training sets of 105 flowers (35 per species) and counts their mistakes on the
remaining 45. The three rules are LDA with both discriminant functions, LDA
with LD1 alone, and QDA, which drops the equal-covariance assumption that
Box's M test rejected.

```{r, file="figs/holdout.png", width=7.5, height=4.2, units=in, res=150}
library(MASS); library(ggplot2)
set.seed(1936)
g <- iris$Species
errors <- t(replicate(500, {
  train <- unlist(lapply(split(seq_len(150), g), sample, 35))
  test <- iris[-train, ]
  l <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris[train, ])
  q <- qda(Species ~ ., data = iris[train, ])
  c(`LDA (LD1 and LD2)` = sum(predict(l, test)$class != test$Species),
    `LDA (LD1 only)` = sum(predict(l, test, dimen = 1)$class != test$Species),
    QDA = sum(predict(q, test)$class != test$Species))
}))
d <- data.frame(rule = factor(rep(colnames(errors), each = nrow(errors)), levels = colnames(errors)),
                errors = c(errors))
p <- ggplot(d, aes(factor(errors, levels = 0:max(errors)), fill = rule)) +
  geom_bar(position = position_dodge(preserve = "single"), width = 0.75) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("#3b528b", "#5ec962", "#e3a33b")) +
  labs(x = "Misclassified test flowers (of 45)", y = "Number of splits (of 500)", fill = NULL) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", panel.grid.major.x = element_blank())
print(p)
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figs/holdout.png)
// result_end
\caption(Number of misclassified test flowers in 500 random 70/30 stratified splits, for LDA with both functions, LDA with LD1 alone, and QDA)
\alttext(A grouped bar chart of the number of test errors per split, from 0 to 5. For all three rules most splits have 0 or 1 errors; QDA has fewer splits with 0 errors and more with 2 or 3 than either LDA rule.)

The hold-out results agree with leave-one-out (Figure 9, Table 14). LDA's test
error averages 2.2%. A third of the splits give no errors at all, and only 5 of
the 500 give more than three. The variation between splits, a standard deviation
of 2.0 percentage points, is as large as the error rate itself. A single
train-test split of the iris data can therefore report anything from perfect
classification to 11% error, which is why repeated resampling or leave-one-out
is needed.

```{r, results=markdown}
library(MASS)
g <- iris$Species
# the same 500 splits as Figure 9 (same seed, same draws)
set.seed(1936)
errors <- t(replicate(500, {
  train <- unlist(lapply(split(seq_len(150), g), sample, 35))
  test <- iris[-train, ]
  l <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris[train, ])
  q <- qda(Species ~ ., data = iris[train, ])
  c(sum(predict(l, test)$class != test$Species),
    sum(predict(l, test, dimen = 1)$class != test$Species),
    sum(predict(q, test)$class != test$Species))
}))
full <- lda(Species ~ ., data = iris)
loo_ld1 <- sapply(seq_len(150), function(i)
  as.character(predict(lda(Species ~ ., data = iris[-i, ]), iris[i, ], dimen = 1)$class))
resub <- c(mean(predict(full)$class != g), mean(predict(full, dimen = 1)$class != g),
           mean(predict(qda(Species ~ ., data = iris))$class != g))
loo <- c(mean(lda(Species ~ ., data = iris, CV = TRUE)$class != g), mean(loo_ld1 != g),
         mean(qda(Species ~ ., data = iris, CV = TRUE)$class != g))
pct <- function(x) sprintf("%.2f%%", 100 * x)
knitr::kable(data.frame(c("LDA, LD1 and LD2", "LDA, LD1 only", "QDA"), pct(resub), pct(loo),
                        sprintf("%.2f%% (%.2f)", 100 * colMeans(errors) / 45, 100 * apply(errors, 2, sd) / 45),
                        sprintf("%.0f%%", 100 * colMeans(errors == 0)), colSums(errors > 3)),
             col.names = c("Rule", "Resubstitution", "Leave-one-out", "Hold-out mean (SD)", "Error-free splits",
                           "Splits with > 3 errors"),
             align = "lrrrrr")
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Rule             | Resubstitution | Leave-one-out | Hold-out mean (SD) | Error-free splits | Splits with > 3 errors |
| :--------------- | -------------: | ------------: | -----------------: | ----------------: | ---------------------: |
| LDA, LD1 and LD2 |          2.00% |         2.00% |       2.20% (1.99) |               33% |                      5 |
| LDA, LD1 only    |          1.33% |         2.00% |       2.10% (1.80) |               33% |                      0 |
| QDA              |          2.00% |         2.67% |       2.71% (2.18) |               25% |                     10 |
// result_end
\caption(Error rates of the three rules under each validation scheme: the hold-out columns are over the 500 splits of Figure 9)

Two further points emerge from the comparison. First, the rule that uses LD1
alone does as well as the one that uses both functions. On the training data it
does slightly better. LD2 carries under 1% of the separation, and its small
contribution is not enough to improve prediction for new flowers. Second, QDA
does slightly *worse* than LDA even though LDA's covariance assumption is
clearly false. QDA estimates three 4-by-4 covariance matrices, 30 parameters,
from 35 to 50 flowers per species, and the extra variance of those estimates
outweighs the bias that pooling introduces. This is the usual result for small
samples \citep(hastie2009). A significant Box's M test is not by itself a
reason to abandon LDA. The question is whether a more flexible model predicts
better, and for these data it does not.

> Conclusion

Fisher's linear discriminant reduces the four iris measurements to a single
score that separates the three species almost perfectly. That score, LD1,
carries 99.1% of the between-species separation and is essentially a contrast
of petal size against sepal width. It places I. setosa about 9 within-species
standard deviations from the other two species, far enough that no setosa is
ever misclassified. I. versicolor and I. virginica are separated too, but they
overlap slightly, and all of the errors come from that overlap. Using the
fitted rule on flowers it was not fitted to, three flowers in 150 are
misclassified (2.0% under leave-one-out, 2.2% averaged over 500 random
splits), and the posterior probabilities flag exactly those flowers as
uncertain.

The model's assumptions do not hold exactly. The species' covariance matrices
differ significantly (Box's M, $p = 3.4 \times 10^{-20}$), and setosa's petal
widths are not normal. Neither problem harms classification. QDA, which does
not assume equal covariances, does slightly worse, and the non-normal variable
belongs to the species that is easiest to separate. The posterior
probabilities should still be read as a guide rather than as calibrated
probabilities.

Three caveats apply. The data are small and famously easy, so these error rates
say little about harder problems. The flowers came from a few natural
populations, so the discriminant may not transfer to irises from elsewhere. And
the three species are equally common here by design, which the equal priors
reflect. In a population where one species is rarer, the priors, and with them
the boundaries, should change.

